(Bug #85 - `{f:8.2}` printed 2.5: neither the width nor the precision,
 though `{f:.2}` prints 2.50 on its own. The reader consumed the width,
 found ".2" left over, matched it against no base specifier and dropped
 it, so the precision was never assigned.

 Both halves are now read and both are kept. They compose under the rule
 #71 already states for the width - "the width is the one exception: it
 applies to any value and is ignored where no padding exists for that
 type yet" - so the precision decides the digits and the width decides
 the padding, and each is honoured wherever a primitive for it exists.

 The float rows are unpadded on purpose: coreasm has no float padder,
 which is bug #36's recorded residue and not this entry's to close. A
 whole number HAS a padder, so `{n:8.2}` gets both.)
a float called f is 2.5.
a number called n is 255.
a text called t is "hi".

(A precision alone, unchanged.)
Print "[{f:.2}]".
Print "[{f:.0}]".

(A width and a precision together: the float takes the places and drops
 the padding, exactly as a bare `{f:8}` drops it.)
Print "[{f:8.2}]".

(A whole number takes BOTH: 255 to two places is `255.00`, eight wide.)
Print "[{n:8.2}]".
Print "[{n:08.2}]".

(And the same pair in a text initializer, so the sinks agree.)
a text called captured is "[{n:8.2}]".
Print captured.

(Every spec that already rendered goes on rendering the same way.)
Print "[{f:8}]".
Print "[{f}]".
Print "[{n:8}]".
Print "[{n:08}]".
Print "[{n:x}]".
Print "[{n:04x}]".
Print "[{n:b}]".
Print "[{t:6}]".

(What follows the width has to BE a count; these name no precision.)
Print "[{f:8.}]".
Print "[{f:8.2z}]".
